---
import Shrine from '../layouts/Shrine.astro';
import { LEDGER_REPO_URL } from '../shared/site';
---

<Shrine title="The Solver" description="How to open a sealed guestbook entry: rite-solver squares a number trillions of times in a row, checkpointing as it goes.">
  <section class="center">
    <p class="eyebrow">Ritual II, the other side</p>
    <h1>Opening a seal</h1>
    <p class="dim lede">
      Every entry in the guestbook can be opened by anyone. It costs nothing but time: one squaring after another, each
      waiting for the last, trillions of times. Starting one is a commitment.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>The ritual</h2>
    <ol class="ritual">
      <li>
        <p>Get the solver. It is a small Rust program using GMP, kept in the <a href={LEDGER_REPO_URL}>public ledger</a> next to the rules.</p>
        <pre><code>git clone {LEDGER_REPO_URL}.git
cd {LEDGER_REPO_URL.split('/').pop()}/solver
cargo build --release</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Choose an entry from the <a href="/archive/">archive</a> and begin.</p>
        <pre><code>./target/release/rite-solver open 12</code></pre>
        <p class="dim">
          It fetches the entry, tells you what you are about to undertake, and waits for you to press Enter. Then it
          squares. If this site is gone, read the entry from a ledger file instead:
          <code>rite-solver open 12 --ledger ../ledger/2026.json</code>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Leave it running. Progress is written to <code>rite-checkpoints/</code> every 30 seconds and when you press
        Ctrl-C. Run the same command again, on the same machine or a faster one years later, and it resumes.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>When the last squaring is done, the solver hashes the result, decrypts, and prints the message. You will
        likely be the first person ever to read it, including its author's future self.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Why it cannot be hurried</h2>
    <p>
      An entry is an RSW time-lock puzzle (Rivest, Shamir and Wagner, 1996). Its key is
      <span class="mono">2<sup>2<sup>T</sup></sup> mod N</span>, and the only known way to compute it without the
      factors of N is to square 2 modulo N, T times in a row. Each step needs the previous one, so a thousand
      computers are no faster than one. Only a faster one helps.
    </p>
    <p>
      The writer's browser knew the two primes behind N, and took a shortcut through
      <span class="mono">φ(N)</span> that turns trillions of squarings into a few thousand. Then it forgot them.
    </p>
    <p>
      T is set each year by measuring how fast a fast desktop CPU squares 2048-bit numbers with GMP:
      <code>rite-solver bench</code> prints the rate and the T it implies for each seal. Expect seals to fall early.
      Rivest's LCS35 puzzle of 1999 was meant to take 35 years; it was solved in 2019, once on an ordinary CPU over
      about three and a half years and once with an FPGA in about two months.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      None of this is quantum-safe. A large enough quantum computer could factor N with Shor's algorithm and open
      every seal at once. That, too, is part of the Rite.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Opening by hand</h2>
    <p>The format is plain, so any language with big integers will do. From the rules:</p>
    <pre><code>x = 2
repeat T times:  x = x² mod N
K = SHA-256( x as big-endian bytes, left-padded to the byte length of N )
message = AES-256-GCM-decrypt( key K, iv, ciphertext with its 16-byte tag, no AAD )</code></pre>
  </section>
</Shrine>

<style>
  .lede {
    max-width: 36rem;
    margin: 0 auto 2.5rem;
  }
  .ritual {
    padding-left: 1.4rem;
  }
  .ritual li {
    margin-bottom: 1.4rem;
  }
  .ritual li::marker {
    color: var(--gold);
    font-style: italic;
  }
</style>
